{"name":"Wild Olive","hex":"#9c8042","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wild olive","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several species resembling a typical olive tree from plant families some of which are only very distantly related.\nBontia daphnoides (family Scrophulariaceae; bastard olive)\nSeveral species in the genus Elaeagnus (family Elaeagnaceae), particularly:\nElaeagnus angustifolia (silver berry, oleaster, Persian olive)\nElaeagnus latifolia (oleaster)\nHalesia carolina (family Styracaceae; Carolina silverbell, little silverbell)\nNyssa aquatica, an American swamp-growing tree (family Cornaceae; water tupelo)\nSubspecies of Olea europea, which includes the cultivated olive (family Oleaceae)\nOlea europaea subsp. cuspidata\nOlea europaea subsp. europaea (syn. Olea oleaster)\nCartrema americana (syn. Osmanthus americanus; devilwood, American olive; family Elaeagnaceae)","Bontia daphnoides (family Scrophulariaceae; bastard olive)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wild_olive"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wild olive","blocks":[{"text":"Wild olive is a common name for several plants and may refer to:"},{"items":["Bontia daphnoides","Several species in the genus Elaeagnus, particularly:\nElaeagnus angustifolia\nElaeagnus latifolia","Elaeagnus angustifolia","Elaeagnus latifolia","Halesia carolina","Nyssa aquatica, an American swamp-growing tree","Olea europaea subsp. cuspidata","Olea oleaster, a species related to the cultivated olive tree","Osmanthus americanus"]},{"items":["Elaeagnus angustifolia","Elaeagnus latifolia"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_olive"},{"title":"Olive","blocks":[{"text":"The cultivated olive is a subspecies of subtropical evergreen tree in the family Oleaceae. Modern cultivars are traced primarily to the Near East, Aegean Sea, and Strait of Gibraltar. Olea europaea is the type species for its genus, Olea, which lends its name to the Oleaceae plant family. The olive fruit is classed botanically as a drupe, similar in structure and function to the cherry or peach. The term oil was originally synonymous with olive oil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Olivesfromjordan.jpg/330px-Olivesfromjordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive"},{"title":"Olive (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Olive, also called olive green, is a dark shade of yellowish green, like that of unripe or green olives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg/330px-Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg/500px-Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg/1280px-Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg/500px-Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOliveira_(Olea_europae